# Notation and an elementary reduction lemma (worker notes, not refereed)

Setup. n = k-1 nonzero integer speeds 0 < v_1 < ... < v_n, gcd = 1 (standard
reduction: Wills/Cusick; the stationary runner is the lonely one, speeds may be
taken integral by continuity/density, and dilating all speeds does not change
anything below). ||x|| = distance from x to the nearest integer.

  ML(v)  := max_{t in R} min_i ||t v_i||            (maximum loneliness)
  LRC_n  :  ML(v) >= 1/(n+1) for every such v.       (known for n <= 6)
  tight  :  ML(v) = 1/(n+1).

Fact (used by all code). f(t) = min_i ||t v_i|| is piecewise linear with slopes
in {+-v_i}; a local maximum of f is either a kink of a single active function
(||t v_i|| = 1/2, i.e. t = a/(2 v_i)) or a crossing of two active functions with
opposite slopes (t v_i = m_i + d, t v_j = m_j - d, i.e. t (v_i + v_j) in Z).
Hence ML(v) = max over t = a/q, q in {v_i + v_j (i<j), 2 v_i}, 0 < a < q, and it
is computed exactly in integer arithmetic:  min_i ||a v_i / q|| >= p/r  iff
r * min(a v_i mod q, q - a v_i mod q) >= p * q for all i.

## Lemma R (top-ratio reduction; elementary, probably folklore)

Assume LRC_{n-1} (with gap 1/n) holds for v' = (v_1, ..., v_{n-1}).
If v_n >= n * v_{n-1}, then ML(v) >= 1/(n+1).

Proof. Choose t0 with ||t0 v_i|| >= 1/n for all i <= n-1. Put
e = 1/(n(n+1) v_{n-1}) and W = [t0 - e, t0 + e]. For t in W and i <= n-1,
||t v_i|| >= ||t0 v_i|| - |t - t0| v_i >= 1/n - e v_{n-1} = 1/n - 1/(n(n+1)) = 1/(n+1).
As t runs over W, t v_n mod 1 sweeps a closed arc of length 2 e v_n =
2 v_n / (n(n+1) v_{n-1}) >= 2/(n+1). The set of positions where runner n is
NOT at distance >= 1/(n+1) from the origin is the open arc (-1/(n+1), 1/(n+1))
of length 2/(n+1); a closed arc of length >= 2/(n+1) cannot lie inside it.
So some t in W has ||t v_n|| >= 1/(n+1) as well.                            QED

Consequences.
* For n = 7 the hypothesis is the Barajas-Serra theorem (LRC_6). Hence to
  verify LRC_7 for all speed vectors with v_6 <= B2 it suffices to check the
  finitely many vectors with v_6 <= B2 and v_6 < v_7 < 7 v_6 (lrc7_adaptive.c).
* Quantitatively the argument is weak: the single window W around t0 has length
  2/(n(n+1)v_{n-1}). Numerically (lrc7.c, ratio-restricted mode) the much
  stronger statement "v_n >= 2 v_{n-1}  =>  v is not tight" holds in all
  tested ranges, but the natural strengthening "=> ML(v) >= 2/(2n+1)" FAILS at
  n = 7: v = (1,2,3,4,5,7,18) has ML = 3/23 < 2/15 (t = 4/23).
